#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116045} :activity/id #uuid "b360f8db-39eb-5e92-8961-f79b03985c78" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050985 :topic/id #uuid "770e795e-38d4-5b4f-8bd2-bd698bb6794a" :topic/math-academy-id 1686} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116473 :tutorial/content "The derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sec x$ | $\\sec x\\tan x$ |\n| $\\csc x$ | $-\\csc x\\cot x$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $-\\csc^2 x$ |\n\nAs with the standard trigonometric functions, we can now differentiate any combination of the above functions using the rules of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of \n\n$$\ny= \\sec x + \\csc x.\n$$\n\nWe can take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could have computed the derivative of the above function by rewriting it as\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand applying the appropriate derivative rules. However, this would have taken significantly more work! You can save a lot of time by memorizing the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions." :tutorial/id #uuid "edab1db9-4d65-5f51-bae3-88df8fcf9c4d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2629 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "67a2cf2e-cd73-5fbe-afd2-7ccbda8b8270" :step/math-academy-id 12343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116214}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115019 :question/id #uuid "118a33c0-5831-586e-a0a5-e8bd3e1e4c9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb56f0a-ead0-5794-9e33-7fe0e1f87dc6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573943}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"} :step/id #uuid "8b5a6be3-f0ee-5a11-899b-4c0f4b56a9f3" :step/math-academy-id 12345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115020 :question/id #uuid "23b1cfcf-9f46-5274-b07b-66770a827409" :question/math-academy-id "e-3646"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484407} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573884}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"} :step/id #uuid "474fcd01-1e90-567e-bf97-01c21b7fde43" :step/math-academy-id 12346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115021 :question/id #uuid "9b2548d6-87fc-56b1-aa7d-cef328eb4e24" :question/math-academy-id "e-3647"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573894}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"} :step/id #uuid "8a445eb0-fd86-5366-ac85-89b0a9dd9f22" :step/math-academy-id 12347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115022 :question/id #uuid "83b8a6dd-c8c4-5537-ac88-289265752755" :question/math-academy-id "e-3648"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486541} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573900}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8a7ecd00-ae83-5c88-8839-738a06fb168f" :step/math-academy-id 12348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116474 :tutorial/content "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "251c3450-3554-5f82-970a-27a2dfd156fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2630 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Derivatives of Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c3a01dc4-5466-5597-861a-59279cd05ea5" :step/math-academy-id 12349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116214 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116524 :tutorial/content "Trigonometric functions appear very frequently in calculus, so it's extremely important to commit their derivatives to memory.\n\nConveniently, there are some mnemonic tricks that we can use to more easily remember the derivatives of trigonometric functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) & =-{csc}^{2}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the \"co\"-functions (cosine, cosecant, and cotangent) all have negative signs in their derivatives, while the other trigonometric functions (sine, secant, and tangent) do not.\n\nIt's also helpful to realize that functions come in pairs.\n\n- Sine and cosine are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Tangent and secant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Cotangent and cosecant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\nLastly, it's helpful to realize the following:\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, tangent and cotangent have a similar format for their derivatives: the square of the function they're paired with.\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, secant and cosecant have a similar format for their derivatives: the product of themself and the function they're paired with." :tutorial/id #uuid "cf33a5e8-2d8b-5494-b60a-fec2fa3cbe4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4574 :tutorial/title "Mnemonic Tricks"} :step/id #uuid "39d5bd85-a6de-52b6-bc2a-2fd13f7f99ec" :step/math-academy-id 28127 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116046}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115457}]}
